---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const howToSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to Password Protect a ZIP File",
  "description": "Password protect a ZIP file using 7-Zip, Windows, macOS, or FileLocker.",
  "step": [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Create your ZIP archive", "text": "Use your OS tools or 7-Zip to compress your files into a ZIP archive." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Open the FileLocker Tool", "text": "Go to filelocker.online/lock-file." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Select your ZIP file", "text": "Select the ZIP file you want to protect from your device." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Set a password", "text": "Enter a strong password for the protected file." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Download the protected file", "text": "FileLocker encrypts your ZIP and saves it as a .vault file." }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I password protect a ZIP file?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "You can password protect a ZIP file using 7-Zip, Windows built-in compression tools, macOS terminal, or FileLocker. 7-Zip is the most commonly used free tool on Windows. FileLocker provides an additional layer of encryption for any existing ZIP file." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does FileLocker create ZIP files?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. FileLocker does not create ZIP archives. It password-protects any existing file, including ZIP files. If you want to compress files first, use Windows built-in compression, 7-Zip, or macOS tools to create the ZIP, then protect that ZIP file with FileLocker." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is 7-Zip password protection secure?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "7-Zip uses AES-256 encryption for password protection when using the .7z format, which is considered strong. However, ZIP-format encryption in 7-Zip (for .zip files) uses ZipCrypto by default, which is weaker. For the strongest protection, use the .7z format in 7-Zip or use FileLocker on your ZIP file." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is my ZIP file uploaded to a server when I use FileLocker?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. FileLocker processes your ZIP file entirely within your browser on your local device. Your file is never uploaded to a server." }
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout
  title="How to Password Protect a ZIP File | FileLocker"
  description="Learn how to password protect a ZIP file using 7-Zip, Windows, macOS, or FileLocker's browser-based tool. No installation required."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(howToSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <div class="w-full bg-[#f8fafc] min-h-screen py-16 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

      <nav class="flex text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8 font-medium">
        <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-brand-blue">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2">/</span>
        <span class="text-gray-900">Password Protect a ZIP File</span>
      </nav>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-3xl p-8 md:p-12 shadow-sm border border-gray-100">

        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-6 leading-tight">How to Password Protect a ZIP File</h1>

        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-600">
          <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-500 mb-8">
            Need to share a group of files securely? Password protecting a ZIP file is a common way to keep the contents safe during transfer. Here are the most reliable methods for Windows, macOS, and any platform.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Why Password Protect a ZIP File?</h2>
          <p>Compressing files into a ZIP archive saves space and keeps multiple files together. Adding a password ensures that only people with the correct password can open the contents. This is useful for:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Sending sensitive documents over email</li>
            <li>Uploading private files to cloud storage</li>
            <li>Archiving files for secure long-term storage</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">How to Password Protect a ZIP File with FileLocker</h2>
          <p>FileLocker does not create ZIP archives, but it can add strong AES-GCM encryption to any existing ZIP file. The workflow is:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Create your ZIP first.</strong> Use Windows built-in compression, 7-Zip, or macOS tools to compress your files into a ZIP archive.</li>
            <li><strong>Open the FileLocker Tool.</strong> Go to the <a href="/lock-file" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">File Protection tool</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Select your ZIP file.</strong> Choose the ZIP archive you just created.</li>
            <li><strong>Set a password.</strong> Enter a strong password.</li>
            <li><strong>Download the protected file.</strong> FileLocker will encrypt your ZIP file and save it as a <code>.vault</code> file. To open it, use the <a href="/unlock-file" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Unlock Tool</a> with the correct password.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>This approach gives you the compression benefit of ZIP combined with FileLocker's browser-based encryption. Your file is never uploaded to a server.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">How to Password Protect a ZIP File with 7-Zip</h2>
          <p>7-Zip is a free, open-source compression tool for Windows that supports strong AES-256 encryption.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Download and install <strong>7-Zip</strong> from 7-zip.org.</li>
            <li>Right-click the file(s) or folder you want to compress.</li>
            <li>Select <strong>7-Zip → Add to archive…</strong></li>
            <li>In the dialog, set <strong>Archive format</strong> to <code>7z</code> (for AES-256) or <code>zip</code>.</li>
            <li>Enter a password in the <strong>Encryption</strong> section and click OK.</li>
          </ol>
          <p><strong>Note:</strong> When using the ZIP format in 7-Zip, the default encryption is ZipCrypto, which is weaker than AES-256. For stronger protection, use the 7z format or switch to AES-256 in the encryption settings.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">How to Password Protect a ZIP File on Windows</h2>
          <p>Windows 10 and Windows 11 include built-in ZIP compression, but the native Windows tool does not support password protection for ZIP files. To add a password on Windows, use 7-Zip (above) or FileLocker.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">How to Password Protect a ZIP File on macOS</h2>
          <p>macOS does not include a native GUI option to password-protect a ZIP file directly. The most reliable options on Mac are:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Terminal (zip command):</strong> <code>zip -e archive.zip file1.txt file2.txt</code> — macOS will prompt you for a password.</li>
            <li><strong>FileLocker:</strong> Create a ZIP using Finder's built-in compression, then protect it with FileLocker as described above.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">Which Method Should You Use?</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse mt-4">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700">
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">Method</th>
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">Platform</th>
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">Requires Install?</th>
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">Encryption</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100">
                  <td class="p-3">FileLocker</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Any (browser)</td>
                  <td class="p-3">No</td>
                  <td class="p-3">AES-GCM</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100 bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="p-3">7-Zip (.7z format)</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Windows, Linux</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Yes</td>
                  <td class="p-3">AES-256</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100">
                  <td class="p-3">macOS Terminal</td>
                  <td class="p-3">macOS</td>
                  <td class="p-3">No</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Legacy ZipCrypto</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="mt-12 space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4 mt-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">How do I password protect a ZIP file?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">You can use 7-Zip (Windows), macOS Terminal, or FileLocker. 7-Zip is the most commonly used free tool on Windows. FileLocker provides additional encryption for any existing ZIP file directly in your browser.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Does FileLocker create ZIP files?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">No. FileLocker does not create ZIP archives. It password-protects any existing file, including ZIP files. Create the ZIP first using your OS tools or 7-Zip, then protect that ZIP with FileLocker.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Is 7-Zip password protection secure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">7-Zip uses AES-256 when using the .7z format, which is considered strong. For .zip format, it defaults to weaker ZipCrypto. For best security, use the .7z format or protect your file with FileLocker.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Is my ZIP file uploaded when I use FileLocker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">No. FileLocker processes your ZIP file entirely within your browser on your local device. Your file is never uploaded to a server.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-12 text-center border-t border-gray-100 pt-10">
          <a href="/lock-file" class="bg-brand-blue hover:bg-brand-blue-dark text-white font-bold py-4 px-10 rounded-full shadow-lg transition-transform hover:scale-105 active:scale-95 inline-block text-lg">
            Password Protect Your ZIP File
          </a>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
